# Clear messages for group projects

slug: eval-overtime-lecture · mode: live · age band: C · group: 7–30 · total: 50 min · version: 1 · written: 2026-10-20

## Outcome

By the end, each learner **can** write a group-chat message that asks for one thing with a deadline and **has made** a message for their own current class project. Evidence: the teacher reads each message on the learner's sheet and ticks the three criteria.

Prior knowledge assumed: has been in a class group chat. Prerequisite check: hands up if you have been in a group chat for a school project this term.

### Success criteria

- criteria: says what is needed · says who does it · says by when
- model answer or work sample: slide 11 of M1, a strong and a weak message side by side
- shown in: S2

### Expect effort

- line: "Writing it yourself will feel slower than listening. That is the part that teaches you."
- said in: S3

## Story frame

- setting: Room 4B's science fair group, three days before the deadline
- character(s): Jun, who sends long messages nobody answers
- stakes: the poster is still blank on Thursday because nobody knew who was printing it
- callbacks: Jun's blank poster returns in S5 and S6
- humour register: dry, band C, never at a learner's expense; one beat per segment at most

## Game layer

- feedback loop: the three-criteria checklist, ticked by a partner
- progression: one tick per criterion met on the learner's own message
- challenge curve: provided messages in S3, their own project in S4
- choice: learners pick which of their projects to write for
- safe failure: S3 messages are fixed in pairs, nothing is collected

## Segments

| id | min | beat | title | what happens (learner's view) | modalities | media | game | story / humour |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| S1 | 5 | hook | Jun's message | Read Jun's 9-line message and write on a mini whiteboard what he actually wants | do · read | – | predict → reveal | Jun's poster |
| S2 | 20 | learn | How good messages work | The teacher talks through twelve slides on clear writing, purpose, audience, tone, deadlines and examples | see · listen | M1 | – | – |
| S3 | 12 | try | Fix three messages | In pairs, rewrite three provided messages so each passes the checklist | do · read | – | partner ticks | – |
| S4 | 12 | apply | Your own project | Write the message you need to send for your own project this week | do | – | ticks on own message | Jun returns |
| S5 | 5 | check | Partner check | Swap sheets, tick the three criteria, return with one suggestion | do · read | – | ticks | Jun's poster gets printed |
| S6 | 3 | reward | Send or schedule | Send the message or set a reminder to send it after school | do · see | – | three ticks shown | callback |

### Segment detail

#### S1 Jun's message
- instruction: "Read Jun's message. On your whiteboard, write in five words what he wants."
- materials: Jun's message on the board; mini whiteboards and pens
- prepare / what goes wrong: whiteboards on desks before the bell / pens run dry, keep spares on the front desk
- fast / slow / wrong: fast learners write who should do it too / slow learners get the first word ("Someone...") / wrong answers are read out without names
- transition: "Most of you found it hard to say what Jun wants. Let's see why."

#### S2 How good messages work
- instruction: "Listen and look at the slides. We will use all of this in a minute."
- materials: slides M1 (twelve slides)
- prepare / what goes wrong: projector on and tested / the class drifts after slide six
- fast / slow / wrong: n/a, whole-class talk
- transition: "Now you try it with three real messages."

#### S3 Fix three messages
- instruction: "With your partner, rewrite each message so it says what, who and by when."
- materials: worksheet with three messages and the checklist
- prepare / what goes wrong: one worksheet per pair / pairs argue over wording, remind them only the three criteria count
- fast / slow / wrong: fast pairs write a fourth message / slow pairs do two of three / wrong rewrites get one hint: "Who does it?"
- transition: "Now write the one you actually need."

#### S4 Your own project
- instruction: "Write the message you need to send for your project this week. Check it against the three criteria."
- materials: the back of the worksheet
- prepare / what goes wrong: learners without a current project use Jun's / some learners copy the S3 answer, ask them to name their real group
- fast / slow / wrong: fast learners write the follow-up message for Friday / slow learners write one sentence per criterion / wrong: the teacher asks "By when?"
- transition: "Swap with your partner."

#### S5 Partner check
- instruction: "Tick each criterion your partner's message meets. Write one suggestion. Give it back."
- materials: worksheets
- prepare / what goes wrong: pairs fixed in advance / partners only praise, ask for one change
- fast / slow / wrong: fast pairs check a second message / slow pairs check only "by when" / wrong ticks are rechecked by the teacher
- transition: "Last thing: send it."

#### S6 Send or schedule
- instruction: "Send your message now or set a reminder to send it after school."
- materials: phones, if school rules allow; otherwise a paper reminder slip
- prepare / what goes wrong: check the school phone rule / no phone allowed, use the slip
- fast / slow / wrong: n/a
- transition: "Next week: replying to a message that is unclear."

## Misconceptions

| misconception | surfaces in | how it is voiced first | the line that fixes it |
|---|---|---|---|
| A longer message is more polite | S1 | learners predict whether Jun's long message is polite | "Polite is easy to answer." |
| Everyone knows the deadline already | S3 | one provided message has no date | "If it is not in the message, nobody knows it." |

## Check-result branch

- weak result (fewer than 2 of 3 ticks): the teacher rewrites one with the learner during the next lesson's opener
- strong result (3 of 3): learner writes the Friday follow-up
- recorded where: the teacher's class list

## Assignment (the practical)

- task: send the message to your real project group
- uses: the reply is the starting point for next week's lesson
- deliverable: a screenshot or the paper slip with the sent message
- check: the three success criteria
- time: 5 min · optional stretch: write the follow-up

